# std.par: running a function on each element of a List, an array, or a
# slice, on several tasks at once, each taking chunks of the elements in
# turn, or each holding a state of its own. The checker types the
# calls, as it does `task.all`.
#
# - `par.map(?xs, f, cost: c, limit: n)` gives a List of `f(x)` for each
#   `x`, in the order of `xs`.
# - `par.each(!xs, f, cost: c, limit: n)` calls `f(!x)` for each element,
#   which it may write.
# - `par.workers(!states, ?items, f, cost: c)` runs a task for each state, which
#   calls `f(!s, x)` with its own state on the next item it takes, in
#   the order of `items`, until none is left. It gives a List of the
#   values in the order of the items, or nothing when `f` gives nothing. Each
#   state goes to one task, to write, so it must be sendable.
# - `par.push(!t, ?sizes, f, cost: c, limit: n)` pushes `sizes.sum()` slots onto
#   the Table `t` at once, then calls `f(!run, k)` for each run `k`, the
#   next `sizes[k]` of those slots in order, as a `![]T`.
#
# `f` is a closure written in the call, or a function's name. Every task
# reads what the closure captures, so each capture must sync, and none
# is a write view. When `f` can fail, so can the call: the first failure
# cancels the other tasks, and the call fails with it. `limit`, of `map`,
# `each`, and `push`, is how many tasks run at once, by default one per worker; with one, or few
# elements, the calls run in turn on the calling task.
#
# `cost`, of all four, is each element's cost, an `Int` (a negative one
# counts as 0): a closure, `cost: |x| x.len`, or a function's name,
# which the calling task calls on each element as `?T` (on each run's
# index `k` for `push`) before the tasks start, or the costs themselves,
# `cost: ?weights`, one for each element, which panic at another length.
# With it, the tasks take the costliest elements first, each taking its
# share of the cost left: the costliest go out one at a time, and cheap
# ones together at the end. The values stay in the order of the
# elements, and each element is still written by one task. Without it,
# `map`, `each`, and `push` hand out chunks of the same size, and
# `workers` the items in their order.
